Z||000000||87ECO5||09-04-2001||10:06 PM||||Painting a house ... can you paint brick?<P>Getting ready to put a coat of paint on the house.  Currently, the (two-story) house is red brick on the lower level, and white aluminum siding on the upper level.<P>Anyway, we've found a color scheme we'd like to go with but the red brick doesn't fit with it.<P>So, can you paint brick?  Does it require special paint?  Are there problems with doing this?<P>TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||banditbalz||09-04-2001||10:14 PM||||uhmm, see 2 posts down....<P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!<p>[This message has been edited by banditbalz (edited 09-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||09-04-2001||10:15 PM||||Hey, that's what we're doing with our house pretty soon. The bottom part is about 1 1/2 feet of brick, and the whole porch is brick. We're planning on painting all the brick a blueish-grey color. So I'm guessing it should work. Not sure what kind of paint we're using. I'd have to ask my dad. If you get your house done first, could you give us some pointers?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">        <BR>^^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^^<BR><LI><FONT SIZE="1">84 SE (FOR SALE)*sale pending*<BR><LI>85 Coupe (parts car)($50)*sale pending*<BR><LI>86 SC (FOR SALE)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||banditbalz||09-04-2001||10:18 PM||||I worked for ICI/Glidden Paints for 11 years. Exterior brick can be painted with Acrylic paint (not Alkyd!). Use 100% acrylic for better expansion of paint through various temps. Primer is not necassary. A Concrete block fill can be used to fill grout lines and imperfections found in brick. Dark colours will fade faster than lighter ones.<P>Happy painting!<P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||09-04-2001||10:22 PM||||I always thought that one of the nice things about brick is that you don't have to paint it!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||LZeitgeist||09-04-2001||10:27 PM||||IMHO, painted brick looks cheesy... at least on the houses I've seen it done on. Gives the impression of flat grey primered body panels on an otherwise nice-looking car. Just looks icky to me. <P>If you've got brick, I'd keep it exposed and find another paint scheme that would use the brick as an accent, rather than a detraction. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||09-04-2001||10:50 PM||||Scott, the front of my house is brick, and it is painted white.  It looks good... much better than the other houses in the neighborhood that aren't painte (being the same floor plan that is... gotta love early '70's track homes).<P>Anyway, the whole house is painted white, with black shutters.  It was done that way b4 I bought it, so I don't know what they used.  Although I am soon to find out, as the house is in need of a a fresh coat of paint.<P>... i know that wasn't any help... but you can ask Lisa, Bob Englert and Formula88 (or is it 88Formula (LZeitgeist's buddy in Raleigh)), they've all seen the house.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000007||frontal lobe||09-05-2001||12:35 AM||||Once it's painted, practically speaking there is no turning back.  Be sure that's what you want to do.<P>Yes, it can be done.  What I wonder is what kind of prep does it need?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||09-05-2001||09:50 AM||||Isn't there a "stain" for bricks? We have a brick fireplace and it has a greenish stain on it. I was thinking of grit blasting the brick and re-staining it a different color. I heard there is a stain for bricks but does anyone know for sure?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||09-05-2001||11:09 AM||||Your house has paint?  Gee, I didn't notice.  Was too busy trying to wrestle the exhaust system into my Grand Am.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I prefer natural brick to painted, but if it's done right, it can look good.  ||||reg||12
